LEAN SIX SIGMA WHITE BELT PROJECT REPORT
DEFINE (Project Charter)
Problem Statement: Our regional logistics operations are experiencing excessive fuel consumption and an inflated carbon footprint due to unplanned route deviations and high vehicle idling times at ports and depots.
Project Objective: To eliminate operational waste (Muda), minimize total fuel oil consumption, and simulate a green supply chain transition by reducing CO2 emissions.
MEASURE (Baseline Data)
Using our Python data analytics script, we successfully quantified the As-Is operational baseline:
Total Fuel Consumed: 367.50 Liters (Diesel)
Total Carbon Footprint: 984.90 kg CO2
Total Wasted Distance: 69.0 KM (Route Deviation)
ANALYZE (Waste Identification / Muda)
Through data stratification, we identified the root causes of our supply chain inefficiencies:
Transportation Waste: Major route deviations were found in SHP-2026-003 (+25 KM) and SHP-2026-005 (+15 KM), indicating poor navigation or lack of standardized routing.
Waiting Waste: SHP-2026-006 sat idling at the Izmir Port for 5.0 hours. Keeping the heavy truck engine running wasted 15 Liters of fuel on a single trip without adding any value to the logistics process.
IMPROVE (To-Be Optimization Strategy)
To reach a lean and green state, we propose the following counter-measures:
GPS-Backed Dynamic Routing: Standardize navigation to match planned distances, eliminating the 69 KM of wasted transportation mileage.
Dynamic Slotting and Appointment Windows: Re-engineer depot entry processes to slash port waiting times to a standard maximum of 0.5 hours per vehicle.
CONTROL (Sustainability Plan)
This Python-based carbon tracking algorithm will be deployed as a standard continuous monitoring tool. Logistics KPIs will be audited quarterly to ensure compliance with green logistics standards and fuel saving goals.

